<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="heme a biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7856" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: HEME_O &quot;Heme &lt;i&gt;o&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| and |FRAME: HEME_A &quot;heme &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| are key compounds of the aerobic respiration in mitochondria and bacteria and used for the dioxygen reduction site of the heme-copper terminal oxidases (e.g. |FRAME: EC-7.1.1.9| and |FRAME: EC-7.1.1.3|). |FRAME: Heme-b &quot;Heme &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| (protoheme IX), the product of the initial heme biosynthetic pathways, is preferred as an electron-accepting heme and used as the dioxygen reduction site only in microaerobic cytochrome &lt;i&gt;bb3&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;cbb3&lt;/i&gt; terminal oxidases. Both |FRAME: HEME_O| and |FRAME: HEME_A| carry a 17-hydroxyethylfarnesyl group at position 2 of pyrrole ring A. They differ only in a formyl group at position 8 of pyrrole ring D that is found in |FRAME: HEME_A|. Like in |FRAME: Cytochromes-B &quot;&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;-type cytochromes&quot;|, heme &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; is often attached to the apoprotein through a coordinate bond between the heme iron and a conserved amino acid side-chain. All known heme &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;-containing oxidases belong to the hemecopper superfamily of terminal oxidases |CITS: [808315]|. |FRAME: HEME_A &quot;Heme &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| is derived from |FRAME: Heme-b| by two integral-membrane enzymes that were initially identified in bacteria, and later found in eukaryotes. The first step, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.141|, consists of the addition of a farnesyl tail to the vinyl group on pyrrole ring A. This enzyme is well characterized. The second reaction, catalyzed by heme &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; synthase, is less well understood. The enzyme oxidizes the methyl substituent on pyrrole ring D to an aldehyde, apparently by two successive hydroxylation. The first hydroxylation forms |FRAME: Heme_I|, the second hydroxylation results in an unstable dihydroxymethyl group, which spontaneously decomposes into a formyl group and a water molecule. However, while heme &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; synthase is strictly dependent on molecular oxygen, the source of the oxygen atom in |FRAME: HEME_A| was shown not to be molecular oxygen. It may be derived from water, but this has not been proven, and the mechanism for such a reaction is not understood |CITS: [15236569]|. In addition, it is still unknown how electrons enter the bacterial enzyme (both a thioredoxin-like protein and menaquinol have been suggested). For a review see |CITS: [22484221]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="zhang" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
